update lr_explanation_choice_notes t set body=v.nb from (values
('e34d0838-18d8-4ccd-8b0d-337355c43b69'::uuid,'015f118d9aba35d7caec6c561e823e14','The argument never weighs academic against nonacademic learning. It limits itself on purpose: the twelve-month schedule is preferred "insofar as academic learning is concerned." Test the presumption: if academic and nonacademic experiences were worth exactly the same, would the argument lose anything? No. It needs no view about their comparative worth.'),
('3549c011-a0fb-4d8c-a7fc-5134a91b642c'::uuid,'87e03991eaade35120dff6bd3051b4ee','The two groups here, children on a nine-month schedule and children on a twelve-month schedule, differ in exactly the way the argument cares about: how the breaks are arranged. That distinction is the subject of the argument, not an arbitrary line. The problem is what the author assumes about one side, not where the line is drawn.'),
('abf5c035-ed10-43f4-80e2-0a928c7019d1'::uuid,'f82a6cd5a82303244c2d2290de63db38','This matches the prediction in formal words. The "two situations" are the two schedules. The "undesirable result" is forgetting schooling over a break. The evidence ties that result to the nine-month schedule, and the author takes for granted that it belongs to that schedule alone, so the month-long breaks are assumed to be safe.

When a comparison has evidence about only one option, check whether the argument quietly assumes the other option is free of the problem. That unstated "only" is what an answer like this one names.'),
('5e9e56b6-b330-429a-a95c-1978c233a16e'::uuid,'653a4aadac0c1a76d1592bea47c6cf31','Questioning the sample attacks the one part of the argument that has evidence behind it. The research only needs to describe children in nine-month schools, and nothing suggests it doesn''t. Grant that the data is perfectly representative and the argument still fails, because no one has shown that month-long breaks avoid the same forgetting.'),
('0e57e7f7-876d-47a4-86e7-37a3d9d78a20'::uuid,'ca8bcd00c1fb88d9b67cd06bdcf2efff','The author accepts the psychologists'' finding and uses it as the main evidence. That view is never rejected later in the argument. If anything, the argument leans on the research too far, by stretching a finding about summer breaks into a promise about month-long breaks.'),
('32e01438-bb80-4728-81ee-0a40814e08e8'::uuid,'699b6e128479874c1493b4ce8a40b18d','The columnist calls these occurrences "innocuous," which means harmless. The whole complaint is that harmless acts get treated as crimes. So the argument presumes the opposite of this answer: it treats the occurrences as not harmful.'),
('0d6f50c3-bbf9-4503-879d-0310cf72cd97'::uuid,'02d66b588d488920601bb03bfa42e3e1','The columnist never says vagrancy laws produce more innocuous occurrences. The occurrences stay the same; only their legal status changes. This answer invents an increase in the occurrences and then criticizes a causal inference the argument never makes.'),
('9a7649fd-02d9-45ac-9956-c066c97c575e'::uuid,'e2a39a136a473d1df894c86f45d74ffd','Ask whether naming the occurrences would fix anything. Suppose the columnist listed them: napping on a bench, standing on a corner. The argument would still slide from "more acts count as crimes" to "more criminal activity." The vagueness isn''t what breaks the argument.'),
('83218fb4-db52-490b-8e73-95df524be292'::uuid,'c52784f6e1fe090028e9fa43f9d35164','On a "fails to consider" answer, ask whether the idea would weaken. Suppose crime would rise even without vagrancy laws. That says nothing about whether the laws themselves add to the count, which is the columnist''s claim. Other causes of crime leave the argument untouched.'),
('ce414672-98b3-4b32-8fdd-fb51629899a8'::uuid,'0c86ef2a3b7af05aec370eccd92d0ef2','This names the slide we predicted. "An increase in criminal activity" is what the conclusion claims. "The reclassification of certain occurrences as crimes" is all the evidence shows.

When a conclusion reuses a word from the evidence, check whether the word means the same thing in both places. If it doesn''t, the right answer says the argument fails to distinguish the two meanings, just as this one does.'),
('1177d3b7-2e6d-4096-8dd2-a3876ba58b4e'::uuid,'5d53023ab7080102e072aa17510ec7db','The critic never relies on the competitor being right. The argument only says what would follow "If this claim were true." A biased source can still make a claim, and a hypothetical about that claim stands or falls on its own logic. The bias never touches the reasoning.'),
('cf58bda1-3e22-45e9-8c00-3387c0935a20'::uuid,'0f406637165eb9f86dcb0d3d8f310b43','This describes a quality passing from an ingredient to the dish: stale okra, so stale gumbo. The critic does something different. The okra lacks freshness, and the critic concludes the gumbo lacks goodness. Those are two different qualities, so this isn''t the error. The real error is the shift from "best" to "good."'),
('b4c437ea-852d-4294-9b3f-193db0fa2049'::uuid,'d339331c61ceecc10faff7547a32fdf0','This is the tempting one, because it uses necessary and sufficient language and mentions both "best" and "good." Check what the argument does with fresh okra. It treats fresh okra as required for good gumbo: no fresh okra, no good gumbo. That''s treating it as necessary, not sufficient.

To confuse it with a sufficient condition, the critic would have to argue that fresh okra guarantees good gumbo. The critic never says that. The error is a necessary condition moved from one standard to another, not a necessary condition flipped into a sufficient one.'),
('2be07b0a-184d-41e2-b0ea-8a07fb664d5f'::uuid,'2876324e0be5b1c4a70efbcd83882cd3','This matches the prediction. The evidence says fresh okra is necessary for the best seafood gumbo. The conclusion uses fresh okra as if it were necessary for good seafood gumbo.

When a conclusion leans on a requirement from the evidence, check that the requirement is attached to the same thing. Here it moved from "best" to "good," and that move is the flaw this answer describes.'),
('6c2276ea-19e0-4752-90b1-72fb354b9a8b'::uuid,'67760a348e6ad050379a9bab2d336191','The critic''s hypothetical already allows for this. A supplier who "cannot reliably supply" fresh okra may still deliver it on some days, and customers still couldn''t count on getting it. Considering the possibility changes nothing, and it never touches the jump from "best" to "good."'),
('6bd57511-0dfa-420c-aa04-c0e0b4b6f9a6'::uuid,'a9e8e4cd282727e0b6dacb531bb8d9b7','The argument does give a reason anarchy beats total control. Total control "destroys the spirit," while anarchy "allows each individual the freedom to pursue a personal course of happiness." So the claim that the argument makes no attempt to explain this is false. The answer misdescribes the argument.'),
('9a25312d-531c-44b2-a51d-c7257f7d3f3c'::uuid,'cbd72b76eac4bd6e9b2160b6290966c2','The argument names tolerating totalitarian control as an option and then recommends against it. Naming an option is not endorsing it. Nowhere does the anarchist say people should tolerate totalitarian government, so there is no contradiction.'),
('65454df0-03b0-42b4-87eb-f21bec5477f7'::uuid,'9a3e4ca2f9139a2199beaccb8745e032','This reverses the direction. The evidence is that totalitarian control makes life miserable, and the conclusion is that people should fight for anarchy. Check any "infers X from Y" answer: Y must match the evidence and X the conclusion. Here they are swapped.'),
('af5d9a8a-1e44-4e4d-8c0d-cef7451d55e2'::uuid,'01cd6fe6ae8abb96e1261a8f75dcbcc3','The anarchist says "history shows" that total control destroys the spirit. That is an indication of evidence, even if the details aren''t given. This answer says there is no indication at all, which the text contradicts.'),
('d0611d74-d9d1-49f8-9d07-278fbd372de1'::uuid,'50de1e31d51437477fabdb3da72da527','This is the false choice. The first sentence offers only two paths, and the argument needs that to be true: rejecting one path proves the other only when nothing else is available.

When an argument gets to its conclusion by eliminating one option, check whether it has shown the options were the only ones. If it hasn''t, an answer like this one names the flaw.'),
('41e3ba86-2046-4d3c-9b7a-714a6e694947'::uuid,'2215c6290ea81e424900c4ab06c812d4','The argument never claims that people consider cyberspace property to be real estate. It claims only that some words for web sites "evoke ideas" of real estate. And even if many people did consider it real estate, that would be one more fact about how we talk and think, not a reason the law should treat the two alike.'),
('6f96cb51-1680-43fd-8f72-8af700bdecd4'::uuid,'a50a9ebfeecb46d40a045aa46f11b34c','Circular reasoning needs a premise that already assumes the conclusion. Check the premises: some words for web sites evoke real estate, and trespass law usually applies to real estate. Neither says trespass law should cover cyberspace. The conclusion goes beyond them, which is the opposite of circular.'),
('fe327f4b-d92b-4be6-96ac-a884624d6bdb'::uuid,'de248182e38d1b609e8ba546f2d470ae','The tempting part is the first sentence: most lawyers think copyright law is the best protection for web site owners. But that isn''t evidence against extending trespass law. Copyright law could be the best tool while trespass law is still a reasonable addition. The evidence gives weak support for the conclusion, not support for the opposite.'),
('a7470bc8-e738-4186-b44e-458bb387b295'::uuid,'db546d94e43b09d3151d6c927a5a4e3f','This matches the prediction. The "similarities that constitute the analogy" are the shared words "site" and "visiting," and "merely verbal" names exactly what''s wrong with them.

When an argument concludes that two things should be treated alike, ask what similarity it has actually shown, and whether that similarity matters for the treatment it recommends. Here the only similarity is in vocabulary.'),
('d4be0d8a-be23-47f8-ae49-c2fba22756a1'::uuid,'1c7a2c4a40bc55aaba2fdbb26371d78c','The argument mentions expert opinion only to move away from it. Most lawyers favor copyright law, and the argument ends up recommending trespass law. It never defends its view by pointing to what experts believe.'),
('c69683a6-ec91-4a15-b1c8-a5746f49625d'::uuid,'7d1a1c1f58a33b23164c8d3b92d9a6fa','The historian''s point is about a building style: large pillars and paneled facades. Suppose the Oromo used these structures for something other than tombs. The style could still have passed from one culture to the other. What the buildings were used for doesn''t touch the question of who influenced whom.'),
('cf285e65-f822-4053-843f-f3b576dafbdd'::uuid,'227304ea380dc9755565ff35690c1eb2','This flaw needs a timeline: A came before B, so A caused B. The historian gives no such timeline. There''s a date for the Swahili tombs and nothing for the Oromo ones. The real trouble runs the other way: the argument needs the Oromo to come first and never shows it.'),
('d06b0378-d8ef-4185-800a-2801b0e1d690'::uuid,'feb779386e332b7e86f994e5617d58f1','Drawing a narrower conclusion than your evidence allows is caution, not a flaw. And the premises don''t strongly support anything broader than influence "to some extent." The trouble is the direction of the influence, not its size.'),
('6f1a46ce-d1db-4c3b-8e24-c0b2510aeb64'::uuid,'159130c2e0ddded9afe210c95115fe27','This sounds like the classic alternative-explanation answer, so test it. "Takes for granted" means ask whether the argument needs it. Suppose a third civilization did create the first such tombs, passed the style to the Oromo, and the Oromo passed it to the Swahili. The conclusion still holds: Swahili culture was influenced by Oromo culture. The argument doesn''t need to rule out a third civilization, so it doesn''t assume one away.'),
('138c0180-b00d-4676-860d-88f4ea869bb7'::uuid,'b7f584339feeb2577e93b6bb58572215','This is the prediction. Influence runs in a direction, and the conclusion picks one: Oromo to Swahili. That requires the Oromo to have built these tombs first, and the historian offers nothing about when the Oromo built them.

Whenever a conclusion says one group influenced another, ask what establishes the order. If nothing does, the assumption that the source came first is the gap.'),
('a87522a7-6533-4cec-b41e-6c51f40708b2'::uuid,'75ba073b8ce5c65c08981476ecea07b1','Run the "fails to consider" test: would this weaken? Suppose planets are the only objects that reflect light. Then the other objects don''t reflect light. That still says nothing about whether they generate it; they could do neither. The gap about generating light stays exactly where it was.'),
('ed5d2a49-4bf1-4e6b-adf3-482e4502c131'::uuid,'2bd1fe7a53f6bd7cc6a2397caebf364f','The argument is only about this galaxy. Whether this galaxy holds a tiny share of the universe''s objects or most of them, the question is the same: do any of its non-star, non-planet objects generate light? The size of the galaxy''s share doesn''t touch that.'),
('a4752c56-4624-429c-ad28-17383d439f91'::uuid,'0d42f7d963567a92fdad6913f21d2fa0','Objects in other galaxies are outside the argument. The conclusion is about light-generating objects "in this galaxy." Knowing that some objects elsewhere generate light can''t make the conclusion any more or less likely to be true here.'),
('8665cb3c-f38c-41f1-95a7-55b53b5c32a9'::uuid,'7651f4579fc0f462a5ab4852c8afa976','The argument never says light is the only difference between stars and planets. It uses light because that''s what the conclusion is about. Stars and planets could differ in a hundred ways and the gap would remain: nothing shows the other objects generate light.'),
('222e83dd-8235-489b-b1b9-52331375ef09'::uuid,'a60fae19557389355d5a4aee3120a1f2','This names the overlooked possibility. The argument needs some of the non-planet objects to generate light, and it simply assumes they do, as if planets were the only objects that don''t. Say other objects also fail to generate light, and the many non-planet objects no longer prove anything.

On a "fails to consider" answer, ask whether the idea would weaken the argument. This one opens exactly the gap the argument skipped.'),
('25055e28-603e-41ba-a038-2e99a1c467d8'::uuid,'a51006d8832df05bce966458697b7c9b','The argument never mentions "any improvement." Its evidence and its conclusion are both about major improvement. So it can''t be inferring something about major improvement from facts about any improvement. This answer criticizes a step the argument never takes.'),
('d55ccc72-2bc7-40b2-b69f-5b0b79b48d53'::uuid,'d8687bf0c27c6c6acfeb92cc9c5531b3','The argument doesn''t need to know which injuries need short treatment and which need long. It compares the two kinds of practitioner within each length of treatment, and the rates match in both. Knowing why one injury takes six weeks and another takes longer would not reveal any difference between the practitioners.'),
('50ac8dfa-b0b7-402f-af44-9c90e7836fcf'::uuid,'3fa6b315ffe6138c1e96b9f3fbe6a73d','The evidence is records of whether patients "showed major improvement." Nothing says those records come from patients rating their practitioners, so to make this answer hurt we would have to add that assumption ourselves. And a patient could feel more warmly about a specialist while the records still show the same improvement.'),
('d719533f-d6de-486c-9cd6-90c1a21059da'::uuid,'302aa41f8373de18f925b50ede56d541','Percentages already account for group size. If 31% of 100 general-practitioner patients and 31% of 1,000 specialist patients improved, the rates are still equal. Unequal numbers of patients would not, by themselves, make the matching percentages misleading.'),
('5cdd5e95-22eb-4381-8fdc-7e784071f139'::uuid,'d42647904370260310c396490ab69329','This is the hidden-subgroup problem from the numbers above. Equal overall rates can come from each kind of practitioner excelling at a different injury, and then the choice matters a lot for any one patient. On a "fails to consider" answer, ask whether the idea would weaken: here it does, because it shows equal averages can sit on top of real differences.

Whenever an argument moves from equal group results to "the choice won''t matter for you," check whether the group could split into parts where the results differ.')
) v(id,ob,nb) where t.id=v.id and md5(t.body)=v.ob returning t.id;